From 2ca5aca838bb24772a8cf45b86872d44db063d36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rasheed <99349713+gitRasheed@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 02:08:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 54/61] SD tests: tiny relative tolerance; bidirectional tests
 share the pairwise verdict

Dominance verdicts compared curve values exactly, so rounding noise of
order 1e-16 decided them. The textbook mean-preserving spread
x = qnorm(ppoints(100)), y = 2x gave 'NO SSD EXISTS' from NNS.SSD
(LPM1 difference +8.9e-16, mean difference -3.7e-18) but 1 from
NNS.SSD.uni on the same data; shifting both by 0.37 flipped .uni to 0;
x = (0.1, 0.7) vs y = (0, 0.8) gave 'NO SSD' because 0.1 + 0.7 =
0.7999999999999999, while the same data times 10 gave 'X SSD Y'.

The native pairwise test now treats differences up to
64 * DBL_EPSILON * S^d as ties (S = largest |value| of the two samples;
d = 1 for SSD curves and the mean gate, 2 for TSD, a ratio analogue for
continuous FSD); discrete FSD still compares exact ECDF fractions. The
bidirectional NNS.SSD and NNS.FSD (discrete) now take their verdict from
the pairwise test in both directions, as NNS.TSD and continuous NNS.FSD
already do, so the bidirectional and .uni results always agree. The
tolerance is documented in a @note of every SD test (roxygen + Rd).

Before -> after:
  x vs 2x:            NNS.SSD 'NO SSD EXISTS' -> 'X SSD Y' (.uni 1, unchanged)
  x+0.37 vs 2x+0.37:  SSD.uni 0 -> 1; NNS.SSD 'NO SSD' -> 'X SSD Y'
  (0.1,0.7) vs (0,0.8): 'NO SSD EXISTS' -> 'X SSD Y' (= the x10 case)
  300 rounded-normal pairs: NNS.SSD agrees with .uni in 296 -> 300
The 11-asset daily-return efficient sets (degrees 1-3, continuous), the
0/15/15 FSD/SSD/TSD pair counts and the 8 TSD counterexamples (still all
rejected) are unchanged.
